      {
        "heading": "What the claim intake callbacks record must show",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A claim intake callbacks record is only useful if it shows the opening event, the source checked, the field changed, and the approver. Record the claim reference, the policyholder, the document version, the reviewer, and the destination. The observed fact stays separate from the coverage interpretation. A reviewer should be able to follow that path in under a minute. Any missing coverage evidence is marked missing until the source arrives.",
          "The proof bar for claim intake callbacks stays deliberately low. Write the source date and mark a missing value as missing rather than supplying it from memory. When two records conflict, keep both and route the narrow question to the claims owner. Virtual Claims Assistant stores the original source next to the correction note. The adjuster checks the loss date and the next contact date before confirming."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where authority stops",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The assistant may prepare, check, record, and route. The adjuster or claims owner keeps any decision that changes coverage, a payment, a denial, access, or a policyholder commitment. When a claim intake callbacks case raises a fraud signal, a privacy question, a dispute, or a request outside scope, preserve the facts and route it. A deliberate stop protects the claim file when the exception is real. The payment register links the payment to the settlement statement.",
          "The claim intake callbacks file should use the same least-privilege rule as the claims system. Each assistant receives an individual account with permissions limited to the written scope. The claims owner reviews that access when the assignment changes or ends. Virtual Claims Assistant records the review date with the claim intake callbacks file. The adjuster checks the loss date and the next contact date before confirming."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Exceptions that stop the case",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Common claim intake callbacks exceptions include a missing source, disagreeing records, an expired authorization, an absent adjuster, an unsigned form, or work outside the agreed scope. Record the exception and the exact missing field so the next reviewer avoids the same search. A short approved reason list keeps the counts comparable. Keep the source, the date observed, the named owner, and the next check with the record. A proof of loss record shows the signature, the attachments, and the submission date.",
          "If a claim intake callbacks exception touches coverage, a payment, a denial, a fraud signal, or a policyholder commitment, the dependent action waits for the claims owner. Do not infer approval from silence or from a prior similar claim. The resolution should say what was decided and where the evidence lives. Keep the source, the date observed, the named owner, and the next check with the record. The adjuster checks the loss date and the next contact date before confirming."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to verify and close",
        "paragraphs": [
          "To verify claim intake callbacks, inspect the claim file, the policy record, the payment log, the denial letter, or the policyholder-visible message. A sent email or a moved task proves activity, not that the claim record changed. Confirm the field or outcome the work was supposed to affect. If the destination cannot be checked, the case is not complete. A reviewer can rebuild the decision from the file without contacting the original assistant.",
          "The close note names what was checked, who approved it, what changed, and what would reopen the case. A claims owner should be able to read one record later and explain the close without contacting the original assistant. Virtual Claims Assistant files the claim intake callbacks close note with the source evidence. Keep the source, the date observed, the named owner, and the next check with the record. The claim file keeps the policy reference, the source document, and the claims owner with the note."
        ]
      }
